# Monte Carlo estimate of pi: pilot run

## Result

Pilot (`pilot.py`, seeded `random.Random(20261019)`, run unmodified as a `local-compute` job, 540.0 s):

| quantity | value |
|---|---|
| samples | 1 759 200 000 |
| hits in quarter disc | 1 381 655 410 |
| estimate of pi (4 x hits / samples) | **3.1415539** |
| standard error | 3.92e-05 |
| 95% CI (Wald, normal approximation) | **[3.1414772, 3.1416307]** (half-width 7.67e-05) |
| 95% CI (Wilson, cross-check) | [3.1414772, 3.1416306] |
| true pi | 3.1415927 |
| error (estimate - pi) | -3.87e-05 (z = -0.99) |

The interval covers the true value of pi. The Wald interval matches the interval the pilot script itself reports, and the Wilson interval agrees to about 8 digits, as expected at this sample size. A single run shows one draw from the sampling distribution; one covered interval does not establish the coverage rate.

Source: `results/estimate.json`, computed from `results/pilot.json`.

